    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why strings matter"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Almost everything a program reads or shows is text: names, passwords, messages, URLs, file paths, JSON from an API, source code, even DNA (`\"GATTACA\"`). Searching, validating, formatting and comparing text are some of the most common jobs in real software — so they are some of the most common interview questions too."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Interviewers like string questions for another reason: they look easy and hide real decisions. Can you change the string, or must you build a new one? Is the alphabet just 26 lowercase letters, or any character? Does `+=` in a loop quietly cost O(n²)? Getting those details right is what the introduction is for."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "A string is an array of characters — with extra rules"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Everything you know about arrays carries over: characters sit next to each other in memory, `s[i]` is O(1), and a loop visits them in order. Three things are different, and each one changes how you solve problems:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Characters are numbers underneath.** `'a'` is stored as 97, and an **encoding** decides how each character becomes bytes. That's why `c - 'a'` turns a letter into a position 0–25. Next lesson.",
        "**Most languages make strings immutable.** In JavaScript, Python and Java you can't change a character in place; every \"change\" builds a new string. That has a cost, and a fix. The lesson after.",
        "**Strings come with a big toolbox** — slicing, searching, splitting, joining, comparing — and each tool has a price you should know before you use it inside a loop."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The basics in four languages"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Here are the moves every string solution uses — reading the length, reading a character by index, and looping — in one small function that counts the vowels in a string."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function countVowels(s) {\n  let count = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {     // s.length: how many characters\n    const ch = s[i].toLowerCase();          // s[i]: the character at index i\n    if (\"aeiou\".includes(ch)) count++;\n  }\n  return count;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def count_vowels(s):\n    count = 0\n    for ch in s:                          # a for loop visits each character in order\n        if ch.lower() in \"aeiou\":\n            count += 1\n    return count"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int countVowels(String s) {\n    int count = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {      // length() is a method on String\n        char ch = Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(i));   // charAt(i): the character at index i\n        if (\"aeiou\".indexOf(ch) >= 0) count++;\n    }\n    return count;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int countVowels(const string& s) {\n    int count = 0;\n    for (char c : s) {                           // a range-for visits each character in order\n        char ch = tolower(c);\n        if (ch == 'a' || ch == 'e' || ch == 'i' || ch == 'o' || ch == 'u') count++;\n    }\n    return count;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "One pass over n characters: **O(n) time, O(1) space**. Notice the small differences: Java reads characters with `charAt(i)` and calls `length()` as a method; Python loops over characters directly; C++ takes the string by `const string&` so it isn't copied."
